@Tutorial(time: 15) {
    @Intro(title: "Create a simple Reducer") {
        Define a `@Reducer` with a single `State` struct and a static `update` function, then build a SwiftUI view and tests on top of it. This is the Reducer-pattern counterpart to the classic State-and-When tutorial, and the recommended way to start a new Statoscope feature.
    }

    @Section(title: "Create a Reducer") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A Reducer is a plain struct annotated with `@Reducer`. It has no properties and no `self` — it defines a nested `State` struct, a `When` enum, and a `static func update(...)` that turns one into the other. The `@Reducer` macro generates a `Store` class around it that actually holds the state and talks to SwiftUI.

            We'll build the same counter as the classic tutorial, so the two are easy to compare side by side.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create a file `CounterReducer.swift` and import Statoscope.

                @Code(name: "CounterReducer.swift", file: StateAndWhen-Reducer-Counter-01.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Annotate a struct with `@Reducer` and give it a nested `State` struct. Unlike classic Statostore, all of a Reducer's state lives in this one struct — there's nothing scattered across separate `@Published` properties.

                @Code(name: "CounterReducer.swift", file: StateAndWhen-Reducer-Counter-02.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Add the `When` events, exactly as in the classic pattern — sentence-like naming still pays off in tests.

                @Code(name: "CounterReducer.swift", file: StateAndWhen-Reducer-Counter-03.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Declare `update` as a **static** function. It takes the event, the state (`inout`), an `effectsState` for enqueueing side effects, and `dependencies` for dependency injection — no `self`, because there isn't one. We'll leave the body empty for now.

                @Code(name: "CounterReducer.swift", file: StateAndWhen-Reducer-Counter-04.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Now implement the logic. Because `update` is static and pure, this reads like a plain function from `(When, State)` to a new `State` — there's no instance state to accidentally read or leak.

                @Code(name: "CounterReducer.swift", file: StateAndWhen-Reducer-Counter-05.swift)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Create your view") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The macro-generated `Store` class conforms to `ObservableObject`, so SwiftUI observes it exactly like any other observable model — the only difference from the classic pattern is that state reads go through a single `.state` property instead of separate `@Published` properties.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Build the view against `CounterReducer.Store`. Read state via `store.state.viewDisplaysTotalCount`, and send events with `store.send(...)`, just like the classic pattern's `model.send(...)`.

                @Code(name: "CounterView.swift", file: StateAndWhen-Reducer-CounterView-01.swift)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Write tests and acceptance criteria") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The same `GIVEN`/`WHEN`/`THEN` fluent testing API works unchanged for Reducers — construct the `Store` in `GIVEN`, and assert on state with `.THEN(\.state.property, equals:)`. The only syntactic difference from classic tests is that extra `.state.` in every keypath.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Import `StatoscopeTesting` and construct the store with `GIVEN`. Because a Reducer's state is a single struct, `GIVEN` just needs an initial `State()` value — there's no scope-wide setup to worry about.

                @Code(name: "CounterReducerTests.swift", file: StateAndWhen-Reducer-CounterTests-02.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                A second test, and the call to `runTest()` that actually executes the flow. Sending the same event repeatedly is a good way to prove your acceptance criteria hold over more than one step, not just the first.

                @Code(name: "CounterReducerTests.swift", file: StateAndWhen-Reducer-CounterTests-04.swift)
            }

            We've built the same counter feature as the classic tutorial — same state, same events, same acceptance tests — but the update logic is a pure static function operating on one state struct, with no scattered `@Published` properties and no `self` to reenter.

            Move on to the next chapters to see how Effects, Middleware, dependency injection, and scope composition work in the Reducer pattern.
        }
    }
}
